<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ho Chi Minh Trail</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1959–1975</span> · Also known as Truong Son Road</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> Trail was a network of roads, tracks and river routes running from North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> to South Vietnam. North Vietnam used it from 1959 to send troops and supplies to the southern insurgency. Despite massive American bombing, especially of eastern Laos, it remained open and was vital to the communist victory in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-trail.jpg" alt="Ho Chi Minh Trail" width="320" height="526" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HoCMT.jpg: The original uploader was RM Gillespie at English Wikipedia. derivative work: Yellowtailshark · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HoCMT.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
